          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.431,
            0.884,
            0.553
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Substituting $x = 0 ,$ we get 5y = 0, that is, y = 0. So (0, 0) is a solution of the given equation. To get another solution, substitute x = 1. The corresponding value of y is $-\\frac{2}{5}, \\mathrm{~S}0, (1, -\\frac{2}{5})$ is also a solution. In fact, we can substitute any value for x and obtain a corresponding value of y. For example, substituting $x = 5$ we obtain $y = - 2$ . Let us represent these solutions or ordered pairs in the form of a table."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.297,
            0.558,
            0.648,
            0.642
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.652,
            0.883,
            0.698
          ],
          "content": "When we plot the ordered pairs A (0, 0), $\\mathrm { B } \\left( 1 , { \\frac { - 2 } { 5 } } \\right)$ and C (5, –2) we see that they lie on a line."
        },
